<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caiaphas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Joseph ben Caiaphas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph ben Caiaphas was a high priest in the first century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew, Luke and John present him as an organiser of the plot against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and as presiding over the Sanhedrin trial. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, the most reliable source outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, says the Roman prefect Valerius Gratus appointed him in 18 CE and that the legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> Lucius Vitellius later deposed him. His tenure of eighteen years suggests a working relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. In John he is the son-in-law of Annas and argues that it is better for one man to die than for the nation to be destroyed. A limestone ossuary found in 1990 may be his, though some scholars dispute the identification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caiaphas.jpg" alt="Caiaphas" width="330" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Stom · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mattias_Stom,_Christ_before_Caiaphas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The primary sources for his life are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>. The family name appears in different forms: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a> gives it as Kuppai and the Jerusalem Talmud as Nekifi. Caiaphas married a daughter of Annas, who had been high priest from 6 to 15 CE and kept a significant influence over Jewish affairs. Annas and Caiaphas may have sympathised with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>, a movement that drew most of its members from the wealthy Jewish elite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, the most reliable source outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, wrote that Caiaphas was appointed high priest by the Roman prefect Valerius Gratus, after Simon ben Camithus had been deposed, and that the legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Lucius Vitellius the Elder, later deposed him. His term of eighteen years was long, and it suggests that he worked well with the Roman authorities.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> the high priests call a meeting of the Sanhedrin after the raising of Lazarus, fearing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> would destroy the holy place and the nation. Caiaphas argues that it is better for one man to die than for the whole nation to be destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is taken first to Annas and then to Caiaphas, and afterwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate">Pontius Pilate</a>. In Matthew, Caiaphas demands that Jesus say whether he is the Messiah, and they charge him with blasphemy. In Acts 4 he questions Peter and John after the healing of a crippled beggar and orders them to stop speaking of Jesus, which they refuse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In November 1990 workers paving a road south of the Abu Tor neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> found an ornate limestone ossuary inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> with what was read as Joseph son of Caiaphas. Some scholars have challenged the identification, citing the spelling and the lack of any mention of his rank as high priest. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s Inferno he is placed among the hypocrites in the eighth circle of Hell, and he has been portrayed in many films, among them King of Kings of 1927 and The Passion of the Christ of 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caiaphas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
